  <h2>Which one should you pick?</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>You're a beginner and want to be told what to do:</strong> Speak.</li>
    <li><strong>You freeze in front of people and a friendly face helps:</strong> Praktika.</li>
    <li><strong>Your language isn't on anyone else's list:</strong> TalkPal.</li>
    <li><strong>You already speak English and the problem is work meetings:</strong> Fluently.</li>
    <li><strong>People understand your English but your accent bothers you:</strong> ELSA Speak, or BoldVoice for an American accent. More on how scoring works in <a href="/blog/ai-accent-training">AI accent training, explained</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>You understand a lot but go quiet when it's your turn, and you want to see where your sentences break:</strong> Vocele.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    If you're weighing an app against a human tutor, the figure I price Vocele against is $200 to $320 a month for a private tutor twice a week. I compared the two in <a href="/blog/ai-speaking-coach-vs-human-tutor">AI speaking coach vs. human tutor</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>No AI language tutor app is best for everyone; pick by what stops you speaking.</li>
    <li>Test a trial off-script, with a deliberate mistake, before you pay.</li>
    <li>Vocele is iPhone only, teaches nine languages and marks corrections inside your own sentence.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Vocele isn't on the App Store yet. At launch, plans are $14.99, $29.99 or $49.99 a month with a 3-day free trial, and conversation and pronunciation are metered monthly on every plan. The <a href="/pricing">pricing page</a> shows what each plan includes, and the <a href="/fair-use">fair-use page</a> explains the monthly allowances.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
